> Define the masking game before interpreting its answer

The input is one finite NHWC image x. Split its H×W spatial positions into
R×C nonempty rectangular patches. Patch indices follow
p=floor(row·R/H)·C+floor(col·C/W), with row-major indexing. Every channel of a
patch switches together. A binary mask z keeps original pixels where z_p=1
and replaces off patches by a scalar baseline. The prediction callback returns
one fixed target score per masked image; its learned parameters stay fixed.
For a coalition S, define v(S)=f(mask(x,S,baseline)).

This fixed-baseline game is explicit. It does not estimate what missing
features would look like conditionally on the observed ones. In particular,
black rectangles can be unlike training images. Calling its Shapley values
"exact" means all coalitions were evaluated, with floating-point arithmetic.
It does not make the masking choice a causal intervention or establish
faithfulness on natural images. The original SHAP paper also discusses
conditional-expectation games; those are a different missing-feature definition.

For P=RC patches, the Shapley coefficient is

\[
\phi_p=\sum_{S\subseteq N\setminus\{p\}}
\frac{|S|!(P-|S|-1)!}{P!}\,[v(S\cup\{p\})-v(S)].
\]

A subset S precedes p in |S|!(P−|S|−1)! of the P! permutations, which
derives the weight. In any one ordering, marginal contributions telescope
from v(empty) to v(full). Averaging gives efficiency,
sum(phi)=v(full)−v(empty), in exact arithmetic. The implementation reports
the actual floating residual instead of forcing the sum to match. Symmetric
players have interchangeable permutations; a player with zero marginal always
receives zero; and averaging is linear in the game. These are the familiar
symmetry, null-player and additivity properties of Shapley credit.

The native implementation scores each of 2^P coalitions once, in ascending
integer-mask order with patch zero the least significant bit. It then performs
P·2^(P−1) weighted marginal terms. It retains the actual Float64 score table,
uses tensor gathers/reductions, and limits prediction image batches separately.
Enumeration is exponential; P<=20 is enforced, and a configurable evaluation
quota defaults to one million. Zero disables the quota alone. Geometry and
known query count are checked before the first callback. This quota is not a
RAM, wall-time or callback-internal-work bound.

> A local surrogate uses different weights and a different objective

For N perturbations, X has an intercept column and the P binary mask columns.
The native LIME variant minimises

\[
\sum_s w_s\,[f_s-\beta_0-\sum_p\beta_p z_{sp}]^2
+\lambda\sum_p\beta_p^2,\qquad
w_s=\exp[-(\mathrm{fractionOff}(z_s)/\sigma)^2].
\]

Differentiating gives
(XᵀWX+lambda·diag(0,1,...,1)) beta=XᵀWf. Float64 tensor Cholesky
solves this system without an explicit inverse. The first two samples are
full and empty anchors; remaining bits come from a local seeded MT19937-64.
Positive ridge penalises coefficients, leaving the intercept unpenalised.
This custom fraction-off kernel and ridge fit uses every patch. It is neither
sparse feature-selecting LIME nor KernelSHAP's special regression kernel.

Retained masks, scores and proximity weights are the actual fit inputs.
Weighted in-sample R² measures this sampled fit, with zero for constant scores;
it is not held-out fidelity. The report also compares the surrogate's full-mask
score with the actual anchored score. Seed, kernel width, baseline, partition
and target definition can change the answer. Batch size should change only
execution grouping for a deterministic, example-independent callback.

These native explanation APIs require CPU tensors and perform no implicit device
transfer. All explanation operations run under NoGradGuard and return detached results.
The callback can still have external side effects; earlier effects cannot be
rolled back if a later callback throws. Nonfinite or wrongly shaped scores
fail explicitly. The legacy scalar DenseMatrix explainers remain available;
this tensor surface adds batched dispatch and explicit diagnostics, and its
anchored sampling is not a numerical reproduction of the old scalar wrapper.

> The two tensor models and their actual architectures

We retain the original 12×12 thickness-two noisy horizontal/vertical bar task,
with eight valid 3×3 CNN filters, ReLU, local 2×2 pooling, a 32-unit ReLU
hidden layer and two logits. The dense baseline flattens the same 144 pixels
and uses 32 hidden ReLU units. Their learned leaves are explicit tensor
parameters. The diagrams execute each configuration and read actual parameter
counts; the following program performs every training update.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/xai_cnn_architecture.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  namespace n=dm::nn;namespace m=n::models;n::set_num_threads(2);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");std::filesystem::create_directories("figures");
  std::print("| actual model | learned leaves | learned scalars | traced scalars |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(int kind:{1,0}){n::ParameterStore p(1);n::Module model([&](const n::Tensor& x){if(kind)return m::DenseImageBaseline(p,x);const auto y=m::SmallImageCnn(p,x);
      n::model_stage("Shared VALID convolution",x,y.conv_pre);n::model_stage("ReLU",y.conv_pre,y.conv_relu);n::model_stage("Local max 2x2",y.conv_relu,y.pooled);n::model_stage("Flatten",y.pooled,y.flat);n::model_stage("Dense hidden ReLU",y.flat,y.hidden);n::model_stage("Two logits",y.hidden,y.logits);return y.logits;},kind?"Dense image baseline":"CNN for explanation");
    n::ModelPlotOptions o;o.title=kind?"Flattened dense classifier":"CNN: shared filters and local pooling";o.style=n::ModelPlotStyle::Flow;o.detail=n::ModelPlotDetail::Blocks;o.labels.type=n::ModelPlotLabel::Full;o.labels.size=13;o.labels.wrap=28;o.sizing.spatial_scale=n::ModelPlotScale::Linear;o.sizing.channel_scale=n::ModelPlotScale::Linear;o.sizing.minimum_extent=1;o.spacing.layer=48;o.spacing.lane=48;
    const auto figure=model.plot(n::zeros({1,12,12,1}),o);const auto file=kind?"xai_mlp_architecture.svg":"xai_cnn_architecture.svg";figure.save_svg(std::string("build/")+file);if(kind)std::filesystem::copy_file(std::string("build/")+file,std::string("figures/")+file,std::filesystem::copy_options::overwrite_existing);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",kind?"dense":"CNN",p.parameters().size(),p.count(),figure.parameter_count());if(p.count()!=figure.parameter_count())throw std::runtime_error("architecture/store count mismatch");}
}
