\caption(Every row is an independently evaluated coalition game on the same image and fixed target class. Efficiency does not select a baseline, a partition or a score definition. The one-short query limit rejects before any callback.)
\alttext(Two native bar charts show signed attribution totals for eight score-grid-baseline choices and their exact query costs of sixteen or 65,536 images.)

> What is established, and what remains a modelling choice

The small game certifies the interaction split and negative credit. The bar
experiments then exercise explicit tensor fits, held-out predictions, batched
model-agnostic explanations and model-specific signed relevance. Measured
residuals check a mathematical identity for each chosen masking game;
correlations describe agreement between particular maps. Neither observation
proves which pixels caused a natural prediction or justifies deployment.

The native API retains actual query inputs or scores so the explanation can
be audited. Runtime remains exponential for exact coalitions. Grid patches
are deliberately simple spatial groups, and splitting their weights among
pixels is a display/accounting choice. A useful next question for a real
application is whether its masking reference and target score match the
scientific question, rather than whether a heatmap merely looks plausible.

> References

- Ribeiro, Singh and Guestrin (2016), *"Why Should I Trust You?": Explaining
  the Predictions of Any Classifier*, KDD. [Original paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.04938),
  [doi:10.1145/2939672.2939778](https://doi.org/10.1145/2939672.2939778).
  The implementation here uses the stated fixed-grid fraction-off kernel,
  full/empty anchors and ridge fit, without sparse feature selection.
- Lundberg and Lee (2017), *A Unified Approach to Interpreting Model Predictions*,
  NIPS 30. [Official paper](https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper/2017/file/8a20a8621978632d76c43dfd28b67767-Paper.pdf).
  This report computes exact Shapley values of a fixed-baseline patch game;
  it does not implement conditional-expectation SHAP or KernelSHAP regression.
- Shapley (1952), *A Value for N-Person Games*, RAND P-295, the working-paper
  version of the cooperative-game construction.
  [Official record](https://www.rand.org/pubs/papers/P295.html).
- The [converted CNN report](../convolution/cnn_analysis.html) documents the
  shared bar generator, tensor model and stabilised signed z-plus rule, with
  its original Bach et al. and Montavon et al. references and limitations.

